<h2>The situation</h2>
<p>The applicant was under two parallel maintenance obligations: Rs 16,000 per month for his daughters under Section 125 of the Code of Criminal Procedure, and Rs 10,000 per month for his wife under Section 12 of the Protection of Women from Domestic Violence Act, 2005. His case was that his services were terminated while the proceedings were ongoing, leaving him without income, and that a recovery warrant had been issued in the DV proceedings for the accumulated arrears.</p>

<h2>What the court did — and carefully did not do</h2>
<p>The High Court did not reduce the maintenance, did not quash the warrant, and did not decide whether the termination was genuine or what the applicant could now afford. On the limited prayer pressed, it kept the recovery warrant in abeyance for fourteen days so that the applicant could approach the trial court for modification of the orders. Two safeguards ensured the pause could not become a loophole: the protection lapses automatically if no modification application is made within the window, and the trial court is to consider any such application entirely on its own terms — in the judgment&rsquo;s words, &ldquo;the concerned court shall consider the same independently, in accordance with law.&rdquo;</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>The structure of the relief:</strong> a short bridge to the proper forum, not a substantive ruling. High Courts regularly adopt this device where an enforcement step is imminent but the underlying grievance belongs to the trial court.</p>
</div>

<h2>The law on changed circumstances</h2>
<p>A genuine and involuntary loss of income is the classic &ldquo;change in circumstances&rdquo; on which maintenance can be altered. The statutory anchors are:</p>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Regime</th><th>Alteration provision</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 125 CrPC orders</td><td>Section 127 CrPC — now Section 146 BNSS, 2023</td></tr>
  <tr><td>DV Act monetary relief and maintenance</td><td>Section 25(2), DV Act 2005 — alteration, modification or revocation on change of circumstances</td></tr>
  <tr><td>HMA pendente lite and permanent alimony</td><td>Sections 24 and 25(2), Hindu Marriage Act 1955</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The payer who seeks relief must come with evidence: the termination letter, the last drawn salary, bank statements, and a candid affidavit of assets and liabilities. Courts distinguish sharply between genuine incapacity and manufactured unemployment — an able-bodied payer is presumed capable of earning, and a resignation engineered to defeat maintenance will not move the needle.</p>

<h2>Practical sequence for a payer in this position</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>1. Act before enforcement.</strong> File the modification application as soon as the income is lost. Arrears that accrue before any variation are rarely erased.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>2. Full disclosure.</strong> Annex the termination documents and a current affidavit of means. Concealment forfeits the court&rsquo;s sympathy and invites adverse inference.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>3. Offer what is possible.</strong> Courts respond better to a payer who tenders a reduced amount in the interim than to one who stops paying altogether.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>4. If a warrant has issued.</strong> A superior court may pause enforcement briefly — as here — but only as a bridge to the trial court, and on strict timelines.</div>
</div>

<h2>The other side of the ledger</h2>
<p>For the wife and children, the decision confirms that enforcement machinery — including recovery warrants — remains fully available, and that any interruption will be short, conditional and self-expiring. Arrears do not vanish with the payer&rsquo;s job; the liability to pay what has already fallen due can be satisfied only by payment. Recipients facing a modification application should test the claimed incapacity with documents of their own: service records, qualifications, past lifestyle and any alternative income streams of the payer are all legitimate material in opposition.</p>
